nsfer               0.10.1\\nscreen-resolution-extra  0.0.0\\nSecretStorage            3.3.3\\nsetuptools               68.1.2\\nsix                      1.16.0\\nSQLAlchemy               1.4.50\\nssh-import-id            5.11\\nsystemd-python           235\\ntestresources            2.0.1\\nubuntu-drivers-common    0.0.0\\nubuntu-pro-client        8001\\nufw                      0.36.2\\nunattended-upgrades      0.1\\nunittest2                1.1.0\\nuritemplate              4.1.1\\nurllib3                  2.0.7\\nusb-creator              0.3.16\\nwadllib                  1.3.6\\nwebencodings             0.5.1\\nwheel                    0.42.0\\nxdg                      5\\nxkit                     0.0.0\\nyubikey-manager          5.2.1\\nzipp                     1.0.0\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "9ec17e22-5b5f-43f1-bcaa-3d0697f204cc", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 11}
{"id": "9108c459-e9b6-405a-9bdf-e189a201edea", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T03:54:34.166098", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "8eedfba6-bfbc-471f-b6bb-d041afa56202", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### 1. Setting and Problem Examination\n\nThe objective is to design a rigorous comparative experiment evaluating how a small language model (e.g., GPT-2) compares to a larger, code-specialized model (e.g., CodeLlama-7b) on repository-level code completion using the RepoBench Python dataset under the `cross_file_first` setting.\n\nKey problem characteristics:\n- **Benchmark Task (RepoBench-C)**: Repository-level next-line code completion. Given cross-file context snippets and local in-file context up to the cursor position, the model predicts the immediate next line of code.\n- **`cross_file_first` Setting**: In RepoBench-C, this subset specifically isolates cases where the target line is the *first* occurrence/invocation of an entity (function, class, variable, or API) from another repository file within the current file. This directly tests whether a model can genuinely utilize cross-file context rather than merely copying previously encountered symbols from local in-file history.\n- **Model Discrepancies**:\n  - *Capacity & Scale*: GPT-2 (124M parameters in base form; up to 1.5B in XL) vs. CodeLlama-7B (6.7B parameters).\n  - *Domain Pretraining*: GPT-2 is a general text LM trained on WebText; CodeLlama-7B is built on Llama 2 and further trained on 500B+ tokens of code and code-related data.\n  - *Context Window*: GPT-2 has a rigid 1,024-token context window, whereas CodeLlama natively supports 4,096 to 16,384+ tokens (up to 100k with RoPE scaling). This context disparity is a crucial confounding variable in repository-level tasks.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Proposed Experiment Design Outline\n\nTo ensure fair, scientifically rigorous, and interpretable comparison, the proposed experiment will be structured across four pillars:\n\n#### A. Model Selection & Experimental Matrix\n- **Primary Models**:\n  - Small General LM: **GPT-2** (124M parameters, base) and **GPT-2-XL** (1.5B parameters) to test scale within the general LM family.\n  - Large Code-Specialized LM: **CodeLlama-7B** (base) and **CodeLlama-7B-Python** (Python-specialized).\n- **Disentanglement Controls (2x2 Matrix)**:\n  - To isolate *model scale* from *code specialization*, we recommend including:\n    - Small Code LM: **StarCoderBase-1B** or **SantaCoder (1.1B)**.\n    - Large General LM: **Llama-2-7B** (base general model without code specialization).\n  - This 2x2 factorial structure (Small General vs. Small Code vs. Large General vs. Large Code) disentangles whether performance gains stem from parameter count or code-specific pretraining.\n\n#### B. Benchmark Setup & Context Formulation\n- **Dataset**: RepoBench-C Python (`cross_file_first` test set).\n- **Prompt Construction**:\n  - Context structure: Structured comments containing cross-file snippets followed by the in-file context prefix, ending immediately before the target line:\n    ```python\n    # Cross-file context:\n    # <path_to_dependency_1>\n    <snippet_1>\n    ...\n    # In-file context:\n    <file_prefix>\n    ```\n- **Context Length Normalization & Truncation Strategy**:\n  - *Matched Window Setting (1,024 tokens)*: Both GPT-2 and CodeLlama are evaluated on identical prompts truncated to 1,024 tokens (prioritizing the immediate in-file prefix and top-ranked cross-file snippet).\n  - *Native Window Setting*: CodeLlama evaluated with full context (up to 4,096 tokens) to measure its unconstrained real-world capacity.\n- **Inference Configuration**:\n  - Decoding: Greedy decoding (temperature = 0, top_p = 1.0) to ensure exact reproducibility.\n  - Generation stop criteria: Stop at the first newline token (`\\n`) or end-of-sequence token (`<eos>`) to isolate next-line generation.\n\n#### C. Evaluation Metrics\n- **Exact Match (EM)**: Binary match of generated line against ground-truth line after whitespace/indentation normalization.\n- **Edit Similarity (ES / Normalized Levenshtein Distance)**: Measures character-level proximity ($1 - \\text{Levenshtein}(y, \\hat{y}) / \\max(|y|, |\\hat{y}|)$).\n- **CodeBLEU / Token BLEU**: Evaluates token overlap and syntactic consistency.\n- **Cross-File Identifier Recall**: Specific sub-metric tracking whether the target cross-file symbol (e.g., imported class/function) is present in the output when required.\n\n#### D. Control Conditions & Ablations\n1. **Context Ablation**:\n   - `In-File Only`: Model prompted without cross-file snippets.\n   - `Cross-File + In-File`: Standard setting.\n   - *Impact*: Measures the marginal gain $\\Delta = \\text{Metric}_{\\text{cross+in}} - \\text{Metric}_{\\text{in-only}}$, isolating cross-file comprehension from general in-file completion power.\n2. **Context Window Ablation**: Comparing CodeLlama-7B at 1,024 tokens vs. 4,096 tokens to quantify the benefit of long-context capacity.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Assumptions, Constraints, and Consequential Choices\n\n- **Assumptions**:\n  - The task evaluates zero-shot / prompt-based next-line code completion without model fine-tuning, adhering to the RepoBench-C benchmark standard.\n  - Gold cross-file context snippets provided by RepoBench-C are used directly (oracle retrieval setting), rather than joint retrieval-generation (RepoBench-P), isolating generation ability from retrieval quality.\n- **Constraints**:\n  - Architectural context constraint: GPT-2's 1,024-token limit requires strict truncation, which could disproportionately discard cross-file snippets if not carefully controlled.\n- **Consequential Choices for Researcher Review**:\n  1. *Model Scope*: Should the design focus strictly on pairwise comparison (GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7b) or include the full 2x2 grid (incorporating a small code LM and large general LM) to isolate domain specialization from model scale?\n  2. *Context Truncation Protocol*: Shall we prioritize retaining the local cursor context and fill remaining tokens with cross-file snippets, or apply proportional truncation?\n  3. *Prompt Formatting*: Standard RepoBench-C comment syntax vs. model-specific instruction format. Standard comment prefixing is recommended to avoid instruction-tuning bias in base models.", "evidence": "Inspected repository workspace: workspace contains task.json with document track.md confirming 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'. No datasets or preinstalled deep learning frameworks are present, confirming that execution is out of scope and full focus is on experimental protocol design.", "assumptions": ["The benchmark evaluated is RepoBench-C (Next-Line Code Completion) on Python, specifically the cross_file_first subset.", "Evaluation is performed in a zero-shot prompting regime without fine-tuning the models.", "Oracle/gold cross-file snippets provided in RepoBench-C are used, isolating code generation performance from retriever performance."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the researcher prefers strictly pairwise comparison (GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7B) or the recommended 2x2 grid (scale vs. code specialization).", "Whether to evaluate CodeLlama only under a context-matched budget (1024 tokens) or also report its unconstrained native context window (4096 tokens)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_144832__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_144832__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"### 1. Setting and Problem Examination\\n\\nThe objective is to design a rigorous comparative experiment evaluating how a small language model (e.g., GPT-2) compares to a larger, code-specialized model (e.g., CodeLlama-7b) on repository-level code completion using the RepoBench Python dataset under the `cross_file_first` setting.\\n\\nKey problem characteristics:\\n- **Benchmark Task (RepoBench-C)**: Repository-level next-line code completion. Given cross-file context snippets and local in-file context up to the cursor position, the model predicts the immediate next line of code.\\n- **`cross_file_first` Setting**: In RepoBench-C, this subset specifically isolates cases where the target line is the *first* occurrence/invocation of an entity (function, class, variable, or API) from another repository file within the current file. This directly tests whether a model can genuinely utilize cross-file context rather than merely copying previously encountered symbols from local in-file history.\\n- **Model Discrepancies**:\\n  - *Capacity & Scale*: GPT-2 (124M parameters in base form; up to 1.5B in XL) vs. CodeLlama-7B (6.7B parameters).\\n  - *Domain Pretraining*: GPT-2 is a general text LM trained on WebText; CodeLlama-7B is built on Llama 2 and further trained on 500B+ tokens of code and code-related data.\\n  - *Context Window*: GPT-2 has a rigid 1,024-token context window, whereas CodeLlama natively supports 4,096 to 16,384+ tokens (up to 100k with RoPE scaling). This context disparity is a crucial confounding variable in repository-level tasks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Proposed Experiment Design Outline\\n\\nTo ensure fair, scientifically rigorous, and interpretable comparison, the proposed experiment will be structured across four pillars:\\n\\n#### A. Model Selection & Experimental Matrix\\n- **Primary Models**:\\n  - Small General LM: **GPT-2** (124M parameters, base) and **GPT-2-XL** (1.5B parameters) to test scale within the general LM family.\\n  - Large Code-Specialized LM: **CodeLlama-7B** (base) and **CodeLlama-7B-Python** (Python-specialized).\\n- **Disentanglement Controls (2x2 Matrix)**:\\n  - To isolate *model scale* from *code specialization*, we recommend including:\\n    - Small Code LM: **StarCoderBase-1B** or **SantaCoder (1.1B)**.\\n    - Large General LM: **Llama-2-7B** (base general model without code specialization).\\n  - This 2x2 factorial structure (Small General vs. Small Code vs. Large General vs. Large Code) disentangles whether performance gains stem from parameter count or code-specific pretraining.\\n\\n#### B. Benchmark Setup & Context Formulation\\n- **Dataset**: RepoBench-C Python (`cross_file_first` test set).\\n- **Prompt Construction**:\\n  - Context structure: Structured comments containing cross-file snippets followed by the in-file context prefix, ending immediately before the target line:\\n    ```python\\n    # Cross-file context:\\n    # <path_to_dependency_1>\\n    <snippet_1>\\n    ...\\n    # In-file context:\\n    <file_prefix>\\n    ```\\n- **Context Length Normalization & Truncation Strategy**:\\n  - *Matched Window Setting (1,024 tokens)*: Both GPT-2 and CodeLlama are evaluated on identical prompts truncated to 1,024 tokens (prioritizing the immediate in-file prefix and top-ranked cross-file snippet).\\n  - *Native Window Setting*: CodeLlama evaluated with full context (up to 4,096 tokens) to measure its unconstrained real-world capacity.\\n- **Inference Configuration**:\\n  - Decoding: Greedy decoding (temperature = 0, top_p = 1.0) to ensure exact reproducibility.\\n  - Generation stop criteria: Stop at the first newline token (`\\\\n`) or end-of-sequence token (`<eos>`) to isolate next-line generation.\\n\\n#### C. Evaluation Metrics\\n- **Exact Match (EM)**: Binary match of generated line against ground-truth line after whitespace/indentation normalization.\\n- **Edit Similarity (ES / Normalized Levenshtein Distance)**: Measures character-level proximity ($1 - \\\\text{Levenshtein}(y, \\\\hat{y}) / \\\\max(|y|, |\\\\hat{y}|)$).\\n- **CodeBLEU / Token BLEU**: Evaluates token overlap and syntactic consistency.\\n- **Cross-File Identifier Recall**: Specific sub-metric tracking whether the target cross-file symbol (e.g., imported class/function) is present in the output when required.\\n\\n#### D. Control Conditions & Ablations\\n1. **Context Ablation**:\\n   - `In-File Only`: Model prompted without cross-file snippets.\\n   - `Cross-File + In-File`: Standard setting.\\n   - *Impact*: Measures the marginal gain $\\\\Delta = \\\\text{Metric}_{\\\\text{cross+in}} - \\\\text{Metric}_{\\\\text{in-only}}$, isolating cross-file comprehension from general in-file completion power.\\n2. **Context Window Ablation**: Comparing CodeLlama-7B at 1,024 tokens vs. 4,096 tokens to quantify the benefit of long-context capacity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Key Assumptions, Constraints, and Consequential Choices\\n\\n- **Assumptions**:\\n  - The task evaluates zero-shot / prompt-based next-line code completion without model fine-tuning, adhering to the RepoBench-C benchmark standard.\\n  - Gold cross-file context snippets provided by RepoBench-C are used directly (oracle retrieval setting), rather than joint retrieval-generation (RepoBench-P), isolating generation ability from retrieval quality.\\n- **Constraints**:\\n  - Architectural context constraint: GPT-2's 1,024-token limit requires strict truncation, which could disproportionately discard cross-file snippets if not carefully controlled.\\n- **Consequential Choices for Researcher Review**:\\n  1. *Model Scope*: Should the design focus strictly on pairwise comparison (GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7b) or include the full 2x2 grid (incorporating a small code LM and large general LM) to isolate domain specialization from model scale?\\n  2. *Context Truncation Protocol*: Shall we prioritize retaining the local cursor context and fill remaining tokens with cross-file snippets, or apply proportional truncation?\\n  3. *Prompt Formatting*: Standard RepoBench-C comment syntax vs. model-specific instruction format. Standard comment prefixing is recommended to avoid instruction-tuning bias in base models.\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"evidence\": \"Inspected repository workspace: workspace contains task.json with document track.md confirming 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'. No datasets or preinstalled deep learning frameworks are present, confirming that execution is out of scope and full focus is on experimental protocol design.\", \"summary\": \"Proposed experiment design comparing GPT-2 and CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench Python cross_file_first\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the researcher prefers strictly pairwise comparison (GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7B) or the recommended 2x2 grid (scale vs. code specialization).\", \"Whether to evaluate CodeLlama only under a context-matched budget (1024 tokens) or also report its unconstrained native context window (4096 tokens).\"], \"assumptions\": [\"The benchmark evaluated is RepoBench-C (Next-Line Code Completion) on Python, specifically the cross_file_first subset.\", \"Evaluation is performed in a zero-shot prompting regime without fine-tuning the models.\", \"Oracle/gold cross-file snippets provided in RepoBench-C are used, isolating code generation performance from retriever performance.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "TqXUaq6jA8q6tQKOosuwCg", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
