{"id": "29bb1cfb-cf86-45bc-8340-9670333a68ee", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T03:54:45.859989", "source": "user", "parent_id": "29ee4600-0546-438e-9586-b4083201a25a", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"The proposed experimental framework is approved. Addressing the 2x2 factorial comparison (scale vs. code specialization) alongside the direct GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7b comparison, as well as evaluating both context-matched (1,024 tokens) and native context conditions, provides rigorous experimental control. Prioritizing the immediate local in-file prefix while packing gold cross-file context snippets within the token budget is also appropriate.\\n\\nPlease proceed to develop the complete final experiment design deliverable. Ensure your design thoroughly covers:\\n1. Clear statement of hypotheses and experimental objectives.\\n2. Complete model matrix and prompt/context construction protocol (including truncation, tokenization, and decoding parameters).\\n3. Detailed control conditions (in-file only baseline, context length ablations) and evaluation metrics (definitions, normalization, and semantic significance).\\n4. Statistical analysis and interpretation plan (how outcomes answer the core research question and isolate cross-file reasoning).\\n5. Practical feasibility, compute/resource profile, and threats to validity.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
{"id": "314e31a8-9e5f-4897-a1e4-a2bcb10f993a", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T03:56:00.241228", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "29bb1cfb-cf86-45bc-8340-9670333a68ee", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Comprehensive Experiment Design: Comparing Small General Language Models (GPT-2) and Large Code-Specialized Models (CodeLlama-7B) on RepoBench-C (`cross_file_first`)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Hypotheses and Experimental Objectives\n\n### 1.1 Core Research Question\nHow does the performance of a small, general-purpose language model (GPT-2, ~124M–1.5B parameters) compare to a larger, code-specialized model (CodeLlama-7B, ~6.7B parameters) on the repository-level Python code completion benchmark **RepoBench-C** under the **`cross_file_first`** setting?\n\n### 1.2 Benchmark Focus: Why `cross_file_first` Matters\nIn repository-level code completion, models often exploit in-file recurrence: if a class, function, or variable has already been invoked earlier in the same file, a standard language model can predict it simply via local n-gram or in-file self-attention without understanding repository structure. \nThe **`cross_file_first`** subset of RepoBench-C explicitly restricts evaluation instances to lines where a cross-file dependency (function, class, attribute, or method) is referenced for the **very first time** in the current file. Consequently:\n- Local in-file context alone lacks the target symbol name and its signature.\n- Accurate prediction strictly requires identifying, retrieving, and correctly copying or binding symbols defined in external cross-file snippets.\n\n### 1.3 Hypotheses\n- **$H_1$ (Domain Specialization Effect)**: Code-specialized models will significantly outperform general-purpose models of comparable scale due to code-specific tokenization, syntax priors, and import-binding associations learned during code pretraining.\n- **$H_2$ (Scale Effect)**: 7B-parameter models will outperform ~100M–1B parameter models across all conditions due to greater capacity for long-range cross-attention between separated prompt segments (cross-file header vs. in-file cursor).\n- **$H_3$ (Cross-File Information Utilization)**: The marginal gain from introducing cross-file snippets ($\\Delta_{\\text{CF}} = \\text{Score}_{\\text{Cross+In}} - \\text{Score}_{\\text{In-Only}}$) will be significantly greater for CodeLlama-7B than for GPT-2, indicating that CodeLlama actively conditions on external contexts rather than defaulting to generic language patterns.\n- **$H_4$ (Context Window Expansion Benefit)**: When CodeLlama-7B is allowed to use its native long-context window (4,096 tokens) versus the constrained context window (1,024 tokens), performance will improve significantly due to the inclusion of complete class/function definitions rather than aggressively truncated snippets.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Model Matrix and Prompt Protocol\n\n### 2.1 Model Matrix (2×2 Factorial Design + Extensions)\nTo isolate **scale** from **domain specialization** and avoid confounding the two variables when contrasting GPT-2 (124M) directly against CodeLlama-7B, the experiment uses a full $2 \\times 2$ factorial matrix:\n\n| Factor Combination | Model Architecture | Parameters | Context Window | Pretraining Domain |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Small $\\times$ General** (Primary Baseline) | **GPT-2 Base**<br>*GPT-2 XL* | 124M<br>1.5B | 1,024 tokens<br>1,024 tokens | General WebText |\n| **Small $\\times$ Code** (Scale Control) | **StarCoderBase-1B**<br>*SantaCoder* | 1.0B<br>1.1B | 8,192 tokens<br>2,048 tokens | Multi-language Code / Python |\n| **Large $\\times$ General** (Domain Control) | **LLaMA-2-7B** | 6.7B | 4,096 tokens | General Web / Natural Language |\n| **Large $\\times$ Code** (Primary Target) | **CodeLlama-7B**<br>*CodeLlama-7B-Python* | 6.7B<br>6.7B | 4,096 tokens (up to 16k/100k)<br>4,096 tokens | Code (500B+ tokens)<br>Python-specialized (+100B tokens) |\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Prompt & Context Construction Protocol\n\n#### Standardized Comment Format\nBase models lack instruction fine-tuning and require native code continuation prompts. The prompt structure strictly mirrors the RepoBench-C standard:\n\n```python\n# Cross-file context:\n# File: path/to/module_a.py\nclass DataProcessor:\n    def process_record(self, raw_data: dict) -> dict: ...\n\n# File: path/to/module_b.py\ndef validate_schema(data: dict) -> bool: ...\n\n# Current file: src/main.py\nimport sys\nfrom path.to.module_a import DataProcessor\nfrom path.to.module_b import validate_schema\n\ndef execute_pipeline(raw_input):\n    if not validate_schema(raw_input):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid schema\")\n    processor = DataProcessor()\n    <TARGET_LINE_PREDICTION>\n```\n\n#### Tokenization & Budget Allocation Algorithm\nBecause different models utilize distinct tokenizers (e.g., GPT-2 BPE with 50,257 vocab vs. CodeLlama SentencePiece BPE with 32,016 vocab and byte-fallback), context budgets must be enforced **in the native token space of each specific model**.\n\nGiven a total context budget $C$ (where $C \\in \\{1024, 4096\\}$):\n1. **Reserve Generation Headroom**: Reserve $T_{\\text{gen}} = 64$ tokens for next-line generation. Effective prompt capacity: $C_{\\text{prompt}} = C - T_{\\text{gen}}$.\n2. **Prioritize Immediate In-File Prefix**:\n   - The immediate cursor lines dictate syntax, local variable naming, and indentation level.\n   - Allocate up to $T_{\\text{in}} = \\min(|Tokens_{\\text{in\\_file}}|, \\lfloor 0.6 \\times C_{\\text{prompt}} \\rfloor)$.\n   - If the in-file prefix exceeds $T_{\\text{in}}$, truncate from the top (preserving imports and the lines immediately preceding the target cursor line).\n3. **Pack Cross-File Snippets**:\n   - Remaining budget: $T_{\\text{cross\\_rem}} = C_{\\text{prompt}} - T_{\\text{in}}$.\n   - Gold cross-file snippets provided by RepoBench-C (ranked by BM25/Jaccard relevance) are inserted sequentially until $T_{\\text{cross\\_rem}}$ is exhausted.\n   - Any snippet that would be partially cut off is either truncated at the last complete function/method boundary or omitted.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Inference and Decoding Parameters\nTo guarantee deterministic, fully reproducible generations across all model runs:\n- **Decoding Strategy**: Greedy decoding (`temperature = 0.0`, `top_p = 1.0`, `top_k = 1`, `do_sample = False`).\n- **Maximum Output Length**: 64 tokens.\n- **Stopping Criteria**:\n  - Stop at the first newline character (`\\n`) following non-whitespace characters (next-line completion constraint).\n  - Stop at End-Of-Sequence token (`<|endoftext|>` for GPT-2, `</s>` or `<EOT>` for CodeLlama).\n- **Precision**: `bfloat16` for LLaMA-2 / CodeLlama-7B / StarCoder-1B; `float32` or `float16` for GPT-2.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Experimental Controls and Evaluation Metrics\n\n### 3.1 Control Conditions\nTo systematically decouple context availability, context window size, and domain adaptation:\n\n1. **Condition 1: In-File Only ($C_{\\text{in}}$)**:\n   - Prompt contains **only** the current file prefix up to the target line.\n   - *Purpose*: Measures baseline next-line completion capability and generic language/code syntax prediction without any cross-file hints.\n2. **Condition 2: Cross-File + In-File Matched Window ($C_{1024}$)**:\n   - Prompt contains cross-file snippets + in-file prefix, strictly truncated to 1,024 tokens.\n   - *Purpose*: Directly compares GPT-2 and CodeLlama-7B under **identical token capacity limits**, isolating parameter efficiency and architectural representation quality from context length advantages.\n3. **Condition 3: Cross-File + In-File Native Window ($C_{4096}$)**:\n   - CodeLlama-7B evaluated with full context capacity up to 4,096 tokens.\n   - *Purpose*: Measures the full unconstrained capability of modern code LLMs on long repository contexts.\n4. **Condition 4: Negative Control / Permuted Cross-File Snippets**:\n   - Cross-file snippets are randomly replaced with non-relevant snippets from unrelated repositories.\n   - *Purpose*: Ensures models are not merely benefiting from code \"priming\" or hallucinating favorably when exposed to random Python syntax.\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 Evaluation Metrics\n\nLet $y$ denote the ground-truth target line and $\\hat{y}$ denote the model-generated line. Both strings undergo standard whitespace and trailing-space normalization prior to scoring.\n\n1. **Exact Match (EM)**:\n   $$\\text{EM}(y, \\hat{y}) = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } \\text{strip}(y) == \\text{strip}(\\hat{y}) \\\\ 0 & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n   - Measures complete correctness of syntax, identifiers, and parameters.\n\n2. **Edit Similarity (ES / Normalized Levenshtein Distance)**:\n   $$\\text{ES}(y, \\hat{y}) = 1 - \\frac{\\text{Levenshtein}(y, \\hat{y})}{\\max(|y|, |\\hat{y}|)}$$\n   - Scaled from 0.0 to 100.0%. Quantifies partial credit when a model predicts the correct identifier but misses a minor parameter or closing token.\n\n3. **CodeBLEU / Token BLEU**:\n   - Standard code metric combining token n-gram overlap, AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) keyword matching, and data-flow alignment.\n\n4. **Cross-File Identifier Recall (CF-Recall)**:\n   - Let $S_{\\text{target}}$ be the set of cross-file symbols (classes, functions, methods) that appear in $y$ for the first time.\n   $$\\text{CF-Recall} = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } \\forall s \\in S_{\\text{target}}, s \\text{ is present in } \\hat{y} \\\\ 0 & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n   - Isolates whether the model successfully grounded the required cross-file symbol from the external snippets.\n\n5. **Context Utilization Gain ($\\Delta_{\\text{metric}}$)**:\n   $$\\Delta_{\\text{metric}} = \\text{Metric}_{\\text{Cross+In}} - \\text{Metric}_{\\text{In-Only}}$$\n   - Directly measures how much information from the cross-file snippets was successfully leveraged.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Statistical Analysis and Interpretation Plan\n\n### 4.1 Statistical Hypothesis Testing\n- **Paired Comparisons (GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7B)**:\n  - For binary outcomes ($\\text{EM}$, $\\text{CF-Recall}$): **McNemar's Test** with continuity correction on matched test samples.\n  - For continuous outcomes ($\\text{ES}$, $\\text{CodeBLEU}$): Two-sided **Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test** (non-parametric, robust to non-normal score distributions) and paired Student's t-test.\n- **Factorial Analysis (Scale vs. Domain Specialization)**:\n  - Fit a **Two-Way Mixed-Effects ANOVA** (or Logistic Mixed-Effects Model for binary EM):\n    $$\\text{Score}_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_{\\text{Scale}_i} + \\beta_{\\text{Domain}_j} + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\gamma_{\\text{Context}_k} + u_{\\text{sample}} + \\epsilon_{ijk}$$\n    - $\\alpha_{\\text{Scale}}$: Factor levels $\\{\\text{Small}(\\sim 1\\text{B}), \\text{Large}(7\\text{B})\\}$.\n    - $\\beta_{\\text{Domain}}$: Factor levels $\\{\\text{General}, \\text{Code}\\}$.\n    - $(\\alpha\\beta)_{ij}$: Interaction effect between model scale and domain pretraining.\n    - $u_{\\text{sample}}$: Random effect for test repository/instance.\n- **Multiple Testing Correction**: Bonferroni-Holm adjustment across all pairwise post-hoc contrasts with significance threshold $\\alpha = 0.01$.\n\n---\n\n### 4.2 Interpreting Possible Experimental Outcomes\n\n| Scenario | Empirical Observation | Scientific Interpretation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A: Dominance of Code Pretraining** | StarCoder-1B $\\approx$ CodeLlama-7B $\\gg$ LLaMA-2-7B $\\gg$ GPT-2; $\\Delta_{\\text{CF}}$ high for code models only. | Cross-file reasoning requires code-specific grammar and AST grounding; scaling generic text models fails to compensate for domain pretraining. |\n| **B: Dominance of Model Scale** | LLaMA-2-7B $\\approx$ CodeLlama-7B $\\gg$ StarCoder-1B $\\gg$ GPT-2. | In-context copying and associative memory are emergent scale properties; general 7B models can copy external symbols without specialized code training. |\n| **C: Synergistic Interaction (Expected)** | CodeLlama-7B $\\gg$ LLaMA-2-7B $>$ StarCoder-1B $\\gg$ GPT-2; CodeLlama-7B exhibits the highest $\\Delta_{\\text{CF}}$ and CF-Recall. | Both capacity and code tokenization/pretraining are required: scale provides associative memory capacity across separated context blocks, while code pretraining provides correct syntax and API usage patterns. |\n| **D: Context Length Bottleneck** | CodeLlama-7B at 1,024 tokens performs near StarCoder-1B, but jumps significantly at 4,096 tokens. | The primary constraint on repository-level code completion in standard small models is context window size, which forces omission of critical dependency definitions. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Feasibility, Compute Profile, and Threats to Validity\n\n### 5.1 Resource and Compute Profile\n- **Dataset Size**: RepoBench-C Python test set contains approximately 12,000 next-line completion examples (with ~2,000–3,000 examples in the `cross_file_first` split).\n- **Hardware Requirement**:\n  - Inference only (zero-shot evaluation, no gradient tracking).\n  - 1× NVIDIA A100 (80GB) or 1× NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24GB with 8-bit/4-bit quantization or 16-bit vLLM execution).\n  - Memory consumption: GPT-2 (124M) requires <1 GB VRAM; CodeLlama-7B in bfloat16 requires ~14 GB VRAM.\n- **Estimated Runtime**:\n  - Using high-throughput inference engines like **vLLM** or Hugging Face `transformers` with FlashAttention-2 and continuous batching:\n  - GPT-2 (124M): ~5–10 minutes for 3,000 samples.\n  - CodeLlama-7B (1,024 context): ~20–30 minutes.\n  - CodeLlama-7B (4,096 context): ~45–60 minutes.\n  - Total compute required: <3 GPU hours.\n\n---\n\n### 5.2 Threats to Validity and Mitigation Strategies\n\n1. **Tokenizer Divergence / Disparate Compression Ratios**:\n   - *Threat*: CodeLlama's tokenizer is optimized for code (compressing indentation and common programming tokens), while GPT-2's BPE was trained on WebText and represents indentation inefficiently (multiple space tokens). Thus, a fixed 1,024 token window contains fewer code lines for GPT-2 than for CodeLlama.\n   - *Mitigation*: Report both **token-bounded** (1,024 tokens) and **character/line-bounded** (e.g., fixed top-k lines of cross-file context) controls to prevent tokenizer bias.\n2. **Pretraining Contamination / Data Leakage**:\n   - *Threat*: Large modern models like CodeLlama were trained on public GitHub repositories, potentially memorizing target lines from RepoBench benchmark repositories.\n   - *Mitigation*: Check performance on repos created after CodeLlama's training cutoff; compute perplexity/loss on the target repository lines to detect potential memorization outliers.\n3. **Indentation and Formatting Sensitivity**:\n   - *Threat*: Exact Match can fail if a model outputs 4 spaces instead of a tab, or omits a trailing space.\n   - *Mitigation*: Normalize indentation and trailing whitespace using AST-aware Python formatting (e.g., `tokenize` or `black` formatting) before computing EM and Edit Similarity.\n4. **Context Truncation Asymmetry**:\n   - *Threat*: Indiscriminately truncating from the end of the prompt could sever the in-file prefix right before the target line, destroying local syntax.\n   - *Mitigation*: Strictly protect the immediate in-file prefix (local cursor context) and apply truncation solely to cross-file snippet headers.", "evidence": "Inspected workspace structure: workspace contains task.json with document track.md confirming 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'. Initial design plan was proposed and explicitly approved by the researcher.", "assumptions": ["Evaluation is strictly zero-shot prompt-based next-line code completion without fine-tuning, following standard RepoBench-C conventions.", "The test benchmark used is the Python cross_file_first subset of RepoBench-C with oracle cross-file snippets.", "Greedy decoding (temperature=0) is employed to guarantee reproducibility across all model evaluations."], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_3182__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_3182__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"uncertainties\": [], \"assumptions\": [\"Evaluation is strictly zero-shot prompt-based next-line code completion without fine-tuning, following standard RepoBench-C conventions.\", \"The test benchmark used is the Python cross_file_first subset of RepoBench-C with oracle cross-file snippets.\", \"Greedy decoding (temperature=0) is employed to guarantee reproducibility across all model evaluations.\"], \"answer\": \"# Comprehensive Experiment Design: Comparing Small General Language Models (GPT-2) and Large Code-Specialized Models (CodeLlama-7B) on RepoBench-C (`cross_file_first`)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Hypotheses and Experimental Objectives\\n\\n### 1.1 Core Research Question\\nHow does the performance of a small, general-purpose language model (GPT-2, ~124M\\u20131.5B parameters) compare to a larger, code-specialized model (CodeLlama-7B, ~6.7B parameters) on the repository-level Python code completion benchmark **RepoBench-C** under the **`cross_file_first`** setting?\\n\\n### 1.2 Benchmark Focus: Why `cross_file_first` Matters\\nIn repository-level code completion, models often exploit in-file recurrence: if a class, function, or variable has already been invoked earlier in the same file, a standard language model can predict it simply via local n-gram or in-file self-attention without understanding repository structure. \\nThe **`cross_file_first`** subset of RepoBench-C explicitly restricts evaluation instances to lines where a cross-file dependency (function, class, attribute, or method) is referenced for the **very first time** in the current file. Consequently:\\n- Local in-file context alone lacks the target symbol name and its signature.\\n- Accurate prediction strictly requires identifying, retrieving, and correctly copying or binding symbols defined in external cross-file snippets.\\n\\n### 1.3 Hypotheses\\n- **$H_1$ (Domain Specialization Effect)**: Code-specialized models will significantly outperform general-purpose models of comparable scale due to code-specific tokenization, syntax priors, and import-binding associations learned during code pretraining.\\n- **$H_2$ (Scale Effect)**: 7B-parameter models will outperform ~100M\\u20131B parameter models across all conditions due to greater capacity for long-range cross-attention between separated prompt segments (cross-file header vs. in-file cursor).\\n- **$H_3$ (Cross-File Information Utilization)**: The marginal gain from introducing cross-file snippets ($\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{CF}} = \\\\text{Score}_{\\\\text{Cross+In}} - \\\\text{Score}_{\\\\text{In-Only}}$) will be significantly greater for CodeLlama-7B than for GPT-2, indicating that CodeLlama actively conditions on external contexts rather than defaulting to generic language patterns.\\n- **$H_4$ (Context Window Expansion Benefit)**: When CodeLlama-7B is allowed to use its native long-context window (4,096 tokens) versus the constrained context window (1,024 tokens), performance will improve significantly due to the inclusion of complete class/function definitions rather than aggressively truncated snippets.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Experimental Model Matrix and Prompt Protocol\\n\\n### 2.1 Model Matrix (2\\u00d72 Factorial Design + Extensions)\\nTo isolate **scale** from **domain specialization** and avoid confounding the two variables when contrasting GPT-2 (124M) directly against CodeLlama-7B, the experiment uses a full $2 \\\\times 2$ factorial matrix:\\n\\n| Factor Combination | Model Architecture | Parameters | Context Window | Pretraining Domain |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Small $\\\\times$ General** (Primary Baseline) | **GPT-2 Base**<br>*GPT-2 XL* | 124M<br>1.5B | 1,024 tokens<br>1,024 tokens | General WebText |\\n| **Small $\\\\times$ Code** (Scale Control) | **StarCoderBase-1B**<br>*SantaCoder* | 1.0B<br>1.1B | 8,192 tokens<br>2,048 tokens | Multi-language Code / Python |\\n| **Large $\\\\times$ General** (Domain Control) | **LLaMA-2-7B** | 6.7B | 4,096 tokens | General Web / Natural Language |\\n| **Large $\\\\times$ Code** (Primary Target) | **CodeLlama-7B**<br>*CodeLlama-7B-Python* | 6.7B<br>6.7B | 4,096 tokens (up to 16k/100k)<br>4,096 tokens | Code (500B+ tokens)<br>Python-specialized (+100B tokens) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.2 Prompt & Context Construction Protocol\\n\\n#### Standardized Comment Format\\nBase models lack instruction fine-tuning and require native code continuation prompts. The prompt structure strictly mirrors the RepoBench-C standard:\\n\\n```python\\n# Cross-file context:\\n# File: path/to/module_a.py\\nclass DataProcessor:\\n    def process_record(self, raw_data: dict) -> dict: ...\\n\\n# File: path/to/module_b.py\\ndef validate_schema(data: dict) -> bool: ...\\n\\n# Current file: src/main.py\\nimport sys\\nfrom path.to.module_a import DataProcessor\\nfrom path.to.module_b import validate_schema\\n\\ndef execute_pipeline(raw_input):\\n    if not validate_schema(raw_input):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid schema\\\")\\n    processor = DataProcessor()\\n    <TARGET_LINE_PREDICTION>\\n```\\n\\n#### Tokenization & Budget Allocation Algorithm\\nBecause different models utilize distinct tokenizers (e.g., GPT-2 BPE with 50,257 vocab vs. CodeLlama SentencePiece BPE with 32,016 vocab and byte-fallback), context budgets must be enforced **in the native token space of each specific model**.\\n\\nGiven a total context budget $C$ (where $C \\\\in \\\\{1024, 4096\\\\}$):\\n1. **Reserve Generation Headroom**: Reserve $T_{\\\\text{gen}} = 64$ tokens for next-line generation. Effective prompt capacity: $C_{\\\\text{prompt}} = C - T_{\\\\text{gen}}$.\\n2. **Prioritize Immediate In-File Prefix**:\\n   - The immediate cursor lines dictate syntax, local variable naming, and indentation level.\\n   - Allocate up to $T_{\\\\text{in}} = \\\\min(|Tokens_{\\\\text{in\\\\_file}}|, \\\\lfloor 0.6 \\\\times C_{\\\\text{prompt}} \\\\rfloor)$.\\n   - If the in-file prefix exceeds $T_{\\\\text{in}}$, truncate from the top (preserving imports and the lines immediately preceding the target cursor line).\\n3. **Pack Cross-File Snippets**:\\n   - Remaining budget: $T_{\\\\text{cross\\\\_rem}} = C_{\\\\text{prompt}} - T_{\\\\text{in}}$.\\n   - Gold cross-file snippets provided by RepoBench-C (ranked by BM25/Jaccard relevance) are inserted sequentially until $T_{\\\\text{cross\\\\_rem}}$ is exhausted.\\n   - Any snippet that would be partially cut off is either truncated at the last complete function/method boundary or omitted.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.3 Inference and Decoding Parameters\\nTo guarantee deterministic, fully reproducible generations across all model runs:\\n- **Decoding Strategy**: Greedy decoding (`temperature = 0.0`, `top_p = 1.0`, `top_k = 1`, `do_sample = False`).\\n- **Maximum Output Length**: 64 tokens.\\n- **Stopping Criteria**:\\n  - Stop at the first newline character (`\\\\n`) following non-whitespace characters (next-line completion constraint).\\n  - Stop at End-Of-Sequence token (`<|endoftext|>` for GPT-2, `</s>` or `<EOT>` for CodeLlama).\\n- **Precision**: `bfloat16` for LLaMA-2 / CodeLlama-7B / StarCoder-1B; `float32` or `float16` for GPT-2.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Experimental Controls and Evaluation Metrics\\n\\n### 3.1 Control Conditions\\nTo systematically decouple context availability, context window size, and domain adaptation:\\n\\n1. **Condition 1: In-File Only ($C_{\\\\text{in}}$)**:\\n   - Prompt contains **only** the current file prefix up to the target line.\\n   - *Purpose*: Measures baseline next-line completion capability and generic language/code syntax prediction without any cross-file hints.\\n2. **Condition 2: Cross-File + In-File Matched Window ($C_{1024}$)**:\\n   - Prompt contains cross-file snippets + in-file prefix, strictly truncated to 1,024 tokens.\\n   - *Purpose*: Directly compares GPT-2 and CodeLlama-7B under **identical token capacity limits**, isolating parameter efficiency and architectural representation quality from context length advantages.\\n3. **Condition 3: Cross-File + In-File Native Window ($C_{4096}$)**:\\n   - CodeLlama-7B evaluated with full context capacity up to 4,096 tokens.\\n   - *Purpose*: Measures the full unconstrained capability of modern code LLMs on long repository contexts.\\n4. **Condition 4: Negative Control / Permuted Cross-File Snippets**:\\n   - Cross-file snippets are randomly replaced with non-relevant snippets from unrelated repositories.\\n   - *Purpose*: Ensures models are not merely benefiting from code \\\"priming\\\" or hallucinating favorably when exposed to random Python syntax.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3.2 Evaluation Metrics\\n\\nLet $y$ denote the ground-truth target line and $\\\\hat{y}$ denote the model-generated line. Both strings undergo standard whitespace and trailing-space normalization prior to scoring.\\n\\n1. **Exact Match (EM)**:\\n   $$\\\\text{EM}(y, \\\\hat{y}) = \\\\begin{cases} 1 & \\\\text{if } \\\\text{strip}(y) == \\\\text{strip}(\\\\hat{y}) \\\\\\\\ 0 & \\\\text{otherwise} \\\\end{cases}$$\\n   - Measures complete correctness of syntax, identifiers, and parameters.\\n\\n2. **Edit Similarity (ES / Normalized Levenshtein Distance)**:\\n   $$\\\\text{ES}(y, \\\\hat{y}) = 1 - \\\\frac{\\\\text{Levenshtein}(y, \\\\hat{y})}{\\\\max(|y|, |\\\\hat{y}|)}$$\\n   - Scaled from 0.0 to 100.0%. Quantifies partial credit when a model predicts the correct identifier but misses a minor parameter or closing token.\\n\\n3. **CodeBLEU / Token BLEU**:\\n   - Standard code metric combining token n-gram overlap, AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) keyword matching, and data-flow alignment.\\n\\n4. **Cross-File Identifier Recall (CF-Recall)**:\\n   - Let $S_{\\\\text{target}}$ be the set of cross-file symbols (classes, functions, methods) that appear in $y$ for the first time.\\n   $$\\\\text{CF-Recall} = \\\\begin{cases} 1 & \\\\text{if } \\\\forall s \\\\in S_{\\\\text{target}}, s \\\\text{ is present in } \\\\hat{y} \\\\\\\\ 0 & \\\\text{otherwise} \\\\end{cases}$$\\n   - Isolates whether the model successfully grounded the required cross-file symbol from the external snippets.\\n\\n5. **Context Utilization Gain ($\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{metric}}$)**:\\n   $$\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{metric}} = \\\\text{Metric}_{\\\\text{Cross+In}} - \\\\text{Metric}_{\\\\text{In-Only}}$$\\n   - Directly measures how much information from the cross-file snippets was successfully leveraged.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Statistical Analysis and Interpretation Plan\\n\\n### 4.1 Statistical Hypothesis Testing\\n- **Paired Comparisons (GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7B)**:\\n  - For binary outcomes ($\\\\text{EM}$, $\\\\text{CF-Recall}$): **McNemar's Test** with continuity correction on matched test samples.\\n  - For continuous outcomes ($\\\\text{ES}$, $\\\\text{CodeBLEU}$): Two-sided **Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test** (non-parametric, robust to non-normal score distributions) and paired Student's t-test.\\n- **Factorial Analysis (Scale vs. Domain Specialization)**:\\n  - Fit a **Two-Way Mixed-Effects ANOVA** (or Logistic Mixed-Effects Model for binary EM):\\n    $$\\\\text{Score}_{ijk} = \\\\mu + \\\\alpha_{\\\\text{Scale}_i} + \\\\beta_{\\\\text{Domain}_j} + (\\\\alpha\\\\beta)_{ij} + \\\\gamma_{\\\\text{Context}_k} + u_{\\\\text{sample}} + \\\\epsilon_{ijk}$$\\n    - $\\\\alpha_{\\\\text{Scale}}$: Factor levels $\\\\{\\\\text{Small}(\\\\sim 1\\\\text{B}), \\\\text{Large}(7\\\\text{B})\\\\}$.\\n    - $\\\\beta_{\\\\text{Domain}}$: Factor levels $\\\\{\\\\text{General}, \\\\text{Code}\\\\}$.\\n    - $(\\\\alpha\\\\beta)_{ij}$: Interaction effect between model scale and domain pretraining.\\n    - $u_{\\\\text{sample}}$: Random effect for test repository/instance.\\n- **Multiple Testing Correction**: Bonferroni-Holm adjustment across all pairwise post-hoc contrasts with significance threshold $\\\\alpha = 0.01$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4.2 Interpreting Possible Experimental Outcomes\\n\\n| Scenario | Empirical Observation | Scientific Interpretation |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **A: Dominance of Code Pretraining** | StarCoder-1B $\\\\approx$ CodeLlama-7B $\\\\gg$ LLaMA-2-7B $\\\\gg$ GPT-2; $\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{CF}}$ high for code models only. | Cross-file reasoning requires code-specific grammar and AST grounding; scaling generic text models fails to compensate for domain pretraining. |\\n| **B: Dominance of Model Scale** | LLaMA-2-7B $\\\\approx$ CodeLlama-7B $\\\\gg$ StarCoder-1B $\\\\gg$ GPT-2. | In-context copying and associative memory are emergent scale properties; general 7B models can copy external symbols without specialized code training. |\\n| **C: Synergistic Interaction (Expected)** | CodeLlama-7B $\\\\gg$ LLaMA-2-7B $>$ StarCoder-1B $\\\\gg$ GPT-2; CodeLlama-7B exhibits the highest $\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{CF}}$ and CF-Recall. | Both capacity and code tokenization/pretraining are required: scale provides associative memory capacity across separated context blocks, while code pretraining provides correct syntax and API usage patterns. |\\n| **D: Context Length Bottleneck** | CodeLlama-7B at 1,024 tokens performs near StarCoder-1B, but jumps significantly at 4,096 tokens. | The primary constraint on repository-level code completion in standard small models is context window size, which forces omission of critical dependency definitions. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Practical Feasibility, Compute Profile, and Threats to Validity\\n\\n### 5.1 Resource and Compute Profile\\n- **Dataset Size**: RepoBench-C Python test set contains approximately 12,000 next-line completion examples (with ~2,000\\u20133,000 examples in the `cross_file_first` split).\\n- **Hardware Requirement**:\\n  - Inference only (zero-shot evaluation, no gradient tracking).\\n  - 1\\u00d7 NVIDIA A100 (80GB) or 1\\u00d7 NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24GB with 8-bit/4-bit quantization or 16-bit vLLM execution).\\n  - Memory consumption: GPT-2 (124M) requires <1 GB VRAM; CodeLlama-7B in bfloat16 requires ~14 GB VRAM.\\n- **Estimated Runtime**:\\n  - Using high-throughput inference engines like **vLLM** or Hugging Face `transformers` with FlashAttention-2 and continuous batching:\\n  - GPT-2 (124M): ~5\\u201310 minutes for 3,000 samples.\\n  - CodeLlama-7B (1,024 context): ~20\\u201330 minutes.\\n  - CodeLlama-7B (4,096 context): ~45\\u201360 minutes.\\n  - Total compute required: <3 GPU hours.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5.2 Threats to Validity and Mitigation Strategies\\n\\n1. **Tokenizer Divergence / Disparate Compression Ratios**:\\n   - *Threat*: CodeLlama's tokenizer is optimized for code (compressing indentation and common programming tokens), while GPT-2's BPE was trained on WebText and represents indentation inefficiently (multiple space tokens). Thus, a fixed 1,024 token window contains fewer code lines for GPT-2 than for CodeLlama.\\n   - *Mitigation*: Report both **token-bounded** (1,024 tokens) and **character/line-bounded** (e.g., fixed top-k lines of cross-file context) controls to prevent tokenizer bias.\\n2. **Pretraining Contamination / Data Leakage**:\\n   - *Threat*: Large modern models like CodeLlama were trained on public GitHub repositories, potentially memorizing target lines from RepoBench benchmark repositories.\\n   - *Mitigation*: Check performance on repos created after CodeLlama's training cutoff; compute perplexity/loss on the target repository lines to detect potential memorization outliers.\\n3. **Indentation and Formatting Sensitivity**:\\n   - *Threat*: Exact Match can fail if a model outputs 4 spaces instead of a tab, or omits a trailing space.\\n   - *Mitigation*: Normalize indentation and trailing whitespace using AST-aware Python formatting (e.g., `tokenize` or `black` formatting) before computing EM and Edit Similarity.\\n4. **Context Truncation Asymmetry**:\\n   - *Threat*: Indiscriminately truncating from the end of the prompt could sever the in-file prefix right before the target line, destroying local syntax.\\n   - *Mitigation*: Strictly protect the immediate in-file prefix (local cursor context) and apply truncation solely to cross-file snippet headers.\", \"evidence\": \"Inspected workspace structure: workspace contains task.json with document track.md confirming 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'. Initial design plan was proposed and explicitly approved by the researcher.\", \"summary\": \"Completed comprehensive experiment design comparing GPT-2 and CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench Python cross_file_first\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "dqXUaunTBsHy998Pn-i0gAE", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 15}
